{#
  The calendar view (FSD §8.1, kalenderkonzept.md Teil A) — three columns of
  day blocks, colour-coded by whose appointment it is.

  **Every measurement is the approved mockup's** (`mockups/preview/08-wand-kalender.png`,
  generated by `tools/mockup-gen/pages_wand.py`), taken out of that file rather
  than re-invented:

    columns   three of 805 px with a 40 px gutter, y 32 … 1408 — the 32 px
              margin is P30, not the mockup's 80 (which left 17 % of the
              panel unused for no measurable gain sideways)
    header    the month, 56 px, over the **first column only** [P46]
    foot      legend and any failure note at the **bottom of the third
              column**, 36 px a line with 24 px of air above. No timestamp
              [P38] — a stamp carrying the minute made every image unique and
              turned every scheduled run into a real push
    rail      132 px: 12 px stripe lane, 4 px, 100 px badge, 16 px [P46]
    badge     black (red on Sundays), number 52 px, weekday 30 px, month 26 px
              on the 1st; as tall as the day, at least 98 px
    band      the week, 44 px on palette yellow with 12 px of air, full width
    entry     84 px, plus 38 px for every further title line
    bar       6 px [Festlegung C8] — 2 px is the proven floor, 6 px reads as a
              bar rather than a hairline; the width is configurable
    time      26 px in 185 px (28 px no longer fits beside the title), #333333
    title     32 px starting at +215 px inside the body, wrapping at ~27
              characters — 34 before the rail took its 132 px
    location  24 px, #555555
    empty day the bare badge; no dash, no words  [P46, after P45]

  **The header is back, and it says something else** [P46]. P29 threw the
  mockup's header out because it set "Sunday, August 23" over a first day
  titled "Today · Sunday, August 23" — the same date twice, for 140 px off all
  three columns. This one carries the **month**, the badges carry number and
  weekday, and it stands over the **first column only**, so columns two and
  three still run the full 1.408 px.

  Two further deviations from the mockup, both measured rather than preferred:
  the rule under a day title and the gutter rule are **black**, not the
  mockup's 2 px `#aaaaaa` — a grey hairline dithers into a dotted trail on this
  panel (measured 2026-08-22, 67 % / 33 % black). Grey *text* stays grey; it is
  the thin lines that fall apart, not the areas.

  Nothing is computed in here. Wrapping, block heights and which day lands in
  which column all arrive decided from `calendar_layout.py`, for the reason the
  recipe page learned the hard way: when CSS wraps what Python budgeted, the
  column runs off the bottom of the canvas and is clipped without a word.
#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ language }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>ePaperEngine</title>
    <style>
      html,
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
        overflow: hidden;
        background: #fff;
        color: #000;
        font-family: "DejaVu Sans", sans-serif;
      }
      main {
        box-sizing: border-box;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
        /* No margin at the bottom [P31]: the calendar is a list that runs
           out, not a page with a baseline. */
        padding: 32px 32px 0 32px;
      }
      /* **No box-sizing here.** The 3 px rule sits *under* the 100 px, not
         inside it — with border-box it landed at y = 177 and pulled all three
         columns 3 px up with it (measured on the first real calendar image).
         80 + 100 + 3 + 37 = 220, the y the mockup starts the columns at. */
      /* The legend is broken into lines by calendar_layout.py, not by CSS.
         The foot is anchored to the bottom of its column, so a line more than
         the model reserved would grow *upwards* into the last appointment
         rather than being clipped where nobody notices. */
      /* Right-aligned [P31]: the foot sits at the outer edge of the last
         column, and a block flushed to the page edge reads as a caption rather
         than as an orphaned first column. Anything else in the foot follows
         the legend — a block with two alignments would look like a mistake. */
      .legend .row {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: flex-end;
        gap: 44px;
        height: 36px;
      }
      .legend .who {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 12px;
        font-size: 28px;
        line-height: 36px;
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
      /* 34 × 24, the mockup's swatch. A filled area, so a palette colour lands
         on the panel as itself — the same reason the bars carry only the six
         Spectra primaries. */
      .legend .chip {
        width: 34px;
        height: 24px;
      }
      .stamp {
        font-size: 28px;
        line-height: 36px;
        color: #555555;
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
      /* Bottom of the third column, its height reserved in the model. */
      .foot {
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        bottom: 0;
        text-align: right;
      }
      /* Only drawn when a source failed or there is nothing to show — a note
         nobody needs is 36 px of appointments. Palette red, so the line itself
         carries no dither raster. */
      .notes .line {
        font-size: 28px;
        line-height: 36px;
        color: #dc1e1e;
        white-space: nowrap;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      .columns {
        display: flex;
        gap: 40px;
      }
      .column {
        flex: none;
        width: 805px;
        position: relative;
      }
      /* The clip is a safety net: if the height model in calendar_layout.py is
         ever off, a column stops at its own edge rather than running into the
         foot or off the canvas. Its height is set inline — the third column is
         shorter by exactly what the foot reserves. */
      .inner {
        position: relative;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      /* The month, over the first column only [P46]. */
      .head {
        font-size: 56px;
        line-height: 62px;
        font-weight: bold;
        white-space: nowrap;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      /* The gutter rule, in the middle of the 40 px gap. Black at 2 px for the
         measured reason above; the mockup's grey would be a dotted trail. */
      .column + .column::before {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        left: -21px;
        top: 0;
        bottom: 0;
        width: 2px;
        background: #000;
      }
      /* Every block is positioned out of the model [P46]. The stripe of a
         multi-day appointment crosses day blocks and the gaps between them, so
         no block can draw it and none of them may be in normal flow either —
         a top computed here and a top computed in `calendar_layout.py` would
         disagree the moment one of them changed. */
      .day {
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
      }
      /* The badge. Filled, so both its colours are palette colours: white
         letters knocked out of a dithered ground is the one thing 52 px of
         type does not survive. */
      .badge {
        position: absolute;
        left: 16px;
        top: 0;
        width: 100px;
        background: #000000;
        color: #ffffff;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      /* Sunday is the badge's ground, not a line's colour [P45 → P46]: a whole
         red block is found from across the room. Red off the palette, so it
         carries no dither raster of its own. */
      .badge.sunday {
        background: #dc1e1e;
      }
      .badge .num {
        font-size: 52px;
        line-height: 58px;
        font-weight: bold;
        padding: 2px 0 0 14px;
      }
      .badge .wd {
        font-size: 30px;
        line-height: 36px;
        padding-left: 14px;
      }
      /* Only on the 1st, and only when the header does not already say it. It
         costs nothing: the badge is at least 98 px tall in any case. */
      .badge .mon {
        font-size: 26px;
        line-height: 32px;
        padding-left: 14px;
      }
      /* Everything that is not the badge starts behind the rail. */
      .body {
        position: absolute;
        left: 132px;
        right: 0;
        top: 0;
      }
      /* The week band [P46], in palette yellow [P47]. It shipped once at grey
         200 and measured 63 % white on the real image — on six primaries a grey
         is mostly white, which is why it looked pale. Yellow lands at 93 %
         pure, and it is the only primary no source colour has taken. Black
         text: yellow *text* at 28 px is what FSD §7 excludes. */
      .band {
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        height: 44px;
        background: #f0c81e;
      }
      .band .kw {
        position: absolute;
        left: 14px;
        top: 0;
        font-size: 28px;
        line-height: 44px;
        font-weight: bold;
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
      .band .span {
        position: absolute;
        right: 14px;
        top: 0;
        font-size: 28px;
        line-height: 44px;
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
      /* The stripe of a multi-day appointment, in the lane outside the badge.
         Drawn after the blocks, so it runs *over* any week band it crosses:
         the appointment did not stop at the week boundary either. */
      .stripe {
        position: absolute;
      }
      .entry {
        position: relative;
      }
      /* The colour bar carries the source [Festlegung C8]. It sits beside the
         line rather than tinting it, so it costs no line height and still
         works when a fourth calendar arrives. */
      .bar {
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        top: 0;
      }
      .time {
        position: absolute;
        left: 20px;
        top: 0;
        width: 185px;
        font-size: 26px;
        line-height: 40px;
        color: #333333;
        white-space: nowrap;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      .what {
        position: absolute;
        left: 215px;
        top: -2px;
        right: 0;
      }
      .what .line {
        font-size: 32px;
        line-height: 38px;
      }
      .what .where {
        font-size: 24px;
        line-height: 32px;
        color: #555555;
        white-space: nowrap;
        overflow: hidden;
        text-overflow: ellipsis;
      }
      /* Same marker as the recipe page: what did not fit is said out loud
         rather than clipped in silence [P13/P14]. */
      .cut {
        height: 46px;
        padding-top: 20px;
        font-size: 26px;
        line-height: 26px;
        color: #dc1e1e;
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>

      <div class="columns" style="height: {{ page.column_h }}px">
        {%- for column in page.columns %}
        {%- set ci = loop.index0 %}
        <section class="column">
          {%- if loop.first %}
          <div class="head" style="height: {{ page.head_h }}px">{{ page.header }}</div>
          {%- endif %}
          <div
            class="inner"
            style="height: {{ page.column_h - (page.head_h if loop.first else 0) - (page.foot_h if loop.last else 0) }}px"
          >
            {%- for item in column %}
            {%- if item.kind == "band" %}
            <div class="band" style="top: {{ item.top }}px">
              <span class="kw">{{ item.label }}</span>
              <span class="span">{{ item.span }}</span>
            </div>
            {%- else %}
            <div class="day" style="top: {{ item.top }}px; height: {{ item.badge_h }}px">
              <div
                class="badge{% if item.sunday %} sunday{% endif %}"
                style="height: {{ item.badge_h }}px"
              >
                <div class="num">{{ item.number }}</div>
                <div class="wd">{{ item.weekday }}</div>
                {%- if item.month %}<div class="mon">{{ item.month }}</div>{% endif %}
              </div>
              <div class="body">
                {%- for entry in item.entries %}
                <div class="entry" style="height: {{ entry.height }}px">
                  <span
                    class="bar"
                    style="width: {{ page.bar_px }}px; height: {{ entry.height - 12 }}px; background: {{ entry.color }}"
                  ></span>
                  <span class="time">{{ entry.time }}</span>
                  <div class="what">
                    {%- for line in entry.title_lines %}
                    <div class="line">{{ line }}</div>
                    {%- endfor %}
                    {%- if entry.location %}<div class="where">{{ entry.location }}</div>{% endif %}
                  </div>
                </div>
                {%- endfor %}
                {%- if item.cut %}
                <div class="cut">{{ t("calendar.cut", count=item.cut) }}</div>
                {%- endif %}
              </div>
            </div>
            {%- endif %}
            {%- endfor %}
            {%- for stripe in page.stripes[ci] %}
            <span
              class="stripe"
              style="left: {{ stripe.left }}px; top: {{ stripe.top }}px; width: {{ stripe.width }}px; height: {{ stripe.height }}px; background: {{ stripe.color }}"
            ></span>
            {%- endfor %}
          </div>
          {%- if loop.last and page.foot_h %}
          <div class="foot" style="height: {{ page.foot_h }}px">
            {%- if page.notes %}
            <div class="notes">
              {%- for note in page.notes %}<div class="line">{{ note }}</div>{% endfor %}
            </div>
            {%- endif %}
            <div class="legend">
              {%- for row in page.legend %}
              <div class="row">
                {%- for who in row %}
                <div class="who">
                  <span class="chip" style="background: {{ who.hex }}"></span>{{ who.label }}
                </div>
                {%- endfor %}
              </div>
              {%- endfor %}
            </div>
            {%- if page.stamp %}
            <div class="stamp">{{ page.stamp }}</div>
            {%- endif %}
          </div>
          {%- endif %}
        </section>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
